virtioscsi.device beta (Rust)
=============================

virtioscsi.device is a block device driver for QEMU's virtio-scsi
controller (virtio-scsi-pci and virtio-scsi-pci-non-transitional), for
AmigaOS 4.1 Final Edition running in QEMU. It is a drop-in replacement
for the C virtioscsi.device (VirtualSCSIDevice), written in Rust: the
same name, loaded from the Kickstart, booting the system disk.

It scans targets 0-7 and LUNs 0-7 on each controller, answers the
trackdisk, TD64 and NSD commands, HD_SCSICMD, geometry and disk-change
requests, and keeps up to 16 commands in flight per controller. On QEMU's
AmigaOne, SFS ran 2.3 to 23 times as fast as with the C driver (most on
random 4 KB reads and writes).

virtio exists only in QEMU: this driver is not for real hardware.
Tested on QEMU 11.0.50's AmigaOne machine (-M amigaone), as the driver
of the system disk.


1. Back up first
----------------
This driver runs your system disk. Keep a copy of your kickstart (zip
or folder) and of your disk image before you start, so that you can go
back if the machine does not boot.


2. Install
----------
virtioscsi.device replaces the module of the same name in the Kickstart
folder your boot loader uses:

    Kickstart/virtioscsi.device

- AmigaOS boot loader: copy it over SYS:Kickstart/virtioscsi.device
  (rename the old one first, e.g. to virtioscsi.device.c).
- QEMU with bboot: the Kickstart folder is inside the kickstart zip you
  give QEMU. Unpack the zip, replace Kickstart/virtioscsi.device, and
  zip it again (keep every other entry as it was).

If your Kicklayout has no line for it yet, add, inside the configuration
you boot (before the blank line that ends it):

    MODULE Kickstart/virtioscsi.device

Reboot. Version virtioscsi.device FULL shows 1.2 when it is the Rust
driver.


3. Going back
-------------
Put the old Kickstart/virtioscsi.device (or your saved kickstart zip)
back and reboot.


4. If something goes wrong
--------------------------
The driver reports what it does on the debug output, in lines that
start with "virtioscsi.device". After a problem, in a Shell:

    DumpDebugBuffer >RAM:debug.txt

and report it with your QEMU version and command line (the controller
and disk options).


Source: https://github.com/derfsss/VirtioRust (MIT licence). The C driver
it replaces: https://github.com/derfsss/VirtualSCSIDevice

